# Day log files are strictly append-only: entries are added, never rewritten
# ("Never rewrite past days — corrections are appended as new entries"). Two sessions
# working the same day therefore always append to the same tail, and git treats that
# as a conflict every time. On 2026-08-20 this file was hand-resolved four times in
# one day, always the same way: keep both sides.
#
# `merge=union` does exactly that, automatically. It is safe here *because* the file
# is append-only — union is the wrong tool for a file whose existing lines change.
#
# The trade-off, stated: union never writes conflict markers, so it removes the human
# review point. check_log_shape.py compensates by failing on a duplicated entry, which
# is the way a union merge would go wrong.
wiki/log/[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9].md merge=union

# The month index is NOT union-merged. It is generated from the day files and kept sorted, and
# since 2026-10-12 each row is a plain link to its day: no counts, so a row never changes once
# written and two changes logged on the same day no longer clash here. Two drafts that each start a
# different new day can still clash; never hand-merge it, run `python3 tools/sync_log_index.py`.
